{
  "result": "There is a particular kind of quiet that belongs only to early morning, before the world has fully decided what kind of day it wants to be. It sits in the kitchen while the kettle warms, in the way sunlight falls across the table in thin, patient stripes, and in the small decision to do nothing at all for a few minutes. I like to believe that time moves differently then, not faster or slower, but more honestly, as if it has stopped trying to impress us. Outside, a neighbor's cat negotiates the fence with the seriousness of a diplomat, pausing, reconsidering, and choosing a path that makes perfect sense to no one else. Somewhere a radio murmurs the weather, half prediction and half poetry, promising rain by afternoon and sunshine as if both can be true, which perhaps they can. I make tea that is slightly too strong, because I always do, and then I pretend this is a deliberate choice rather than a habit I refuse to correct. The steam curls upward and briefly writes invisible messages on the window, which I read as encouragement, even though I know it is only water and heat and my imagination working overtime again. Later, the day will ask for plans and explanations and useful decisions, but for now I am content to let the morning remain unfinished, like a sentence that does not need to end to be complete. And that, I think, is enough for any beginning to start.",
